In 2013, the Canadian explorer George Kourounis became the first known person to reach the crater floor. Wearing a heat-reflecting suit and breathing equipment, he was lowered on ropes into the fire.

He collected soil samples from the bottom. Back in a laboratory, scientists found bacteria living in that hot, gassy soil. None of them were found in the surrounding desert. It was a small sign of how tough life can be. Scientists who search for life on other planets were interested, because it shows microbes can survive in places that look hopeless.

{{story:cooking-gas}}

Around 21 June, the northern half of Earth leans most towards the Sun. This is the June solstice, the longest day of the year in the north. Around 21 December comes the December solstice, the shortest day. In between, around 20 March and 22 September, are the [[@india-wonder-sun-temple-modhera|equinoxes]], when day and night are roughly equal everywhere.

Many festivals mark these turning points. Makar Sankranti, in mid-January, celebrates the Sun's journey northward and the lengthening of days. In Gujarat it is famous for [[@kites-festival|kite flying]]. It once fell close to the winter solstice, but because Earth's axis slowly wobbles over thousands of years, the dates have drifted apart.

{{story:kites-festival}}

Bamboo looks like a tree, but it is a grass, a giant cousin of [[@rice|rice]], wheat and sugarcane. Like other grasses, it has hollow, jointed stems and long, narrow leaves. It never forms wood with yearly rings.

For a long time, Indian law treated bamboo as a tree, so farmers needed permits to cut and move bamboo even from their own land. In 2017, the law was changed so that bamboo grown outside forests is no longer counted as a tree. India has one of the largest areas of bamboo in the world, rivalled only by China.

{{story:light-bulb}}

In 1902, a young American engineer named Willis Carrier was asked to help a printing company in Brooklyn, [[@new-york|New York]]. In hot, humid summers, the paper swelled and shrank, so colours printed one after another didn't line up. Carrier built a machine that blew air over cold coils to control the moisture.

It worked, and workers noticed the factory was pleasantly cool. Soon cinemas, shops and offices wanted the same thing. American cinemas advertised cool air in summer, and going to the pictures became a way to escape the heat. Home ACs spread in the USA after the 1950s and in India much later.

{{story:heatwaves}}

Before lifts, few people wanted to climb more than five or six floors, so buildings stayed short. The top floors were often the cheapest, because nobody wanted to walk that far. Safe lifts flipped that. Suddenly the top floors, with the best views and the cleanest air, became the most expensive.

Steel frames and lifts arrived around the same time, and together they made the first [[@skyscrapers|skyscrapers]] possible. Today the Burj Khalifa in [[@dubai|Dubai]] has dozens of lifts, and some of its cars travel about 500 metres in a single trip.

{{story:skyscrapers}}

Coins are rarely made of a single pure metal. Mints use [[alloy|alloys]], mixtures of metals chosen to be hard, cheap and hard to fake. Many Indian coins today are made of ferritic stainless [[@steel|steel]], which is tough and doesn't rust. A one-rupee coin weighs under 4 grams.

In the USA, the penny was about 97.5% zinc with a thin copper skin. By 2024, each one cost about 3.7 cents to make. So in November 2025, the US Mint struck its last pennies for everyday use, after more than 230 years. Many shops now round cash prices to the nearest five cents.

{{story:made-banknotes}}

S.D. Burman's son, Rahul Dev Burman, known as R.D. or Pancham, loved experimenting. He mixed rock, jazz and Latin American rhythms with Indian melodies. He is said to have made sounds by blowing across bottles and rubbing a comb, just to get a new texture.

His music for *Sholay* (1975) and dozens of other films made the 1970s sound young and daring. Many musicians today still borrow his beats.

{{story:bollywood}}

Henna is a small shrub that grows well in hot, dry lands, from North Africa through the Middle East to India. Sojat, a town in the Pali district of Rajasthan, is famous for its henna, and in 2021 Sojat Mehndi received a GI tag. That label means the name can only be used for henna from that area.

Farmers cut the leafy branches, dry them in the sun and grind the leaves into a fine green powder. Mixed with water, lemon juice and sometimes tea or a few drops of oil, the powder becomes a thick paste. Artists roll it into cones, like piping bags for cake icing, and squeeze out thin lines.

{{story:rangoli-symmetry}}

Rulers and wealthy families have funded wells, rest houses and temples for centuries. In the 1800s and 1900s, rich industrialists began giving away huge fortunes. Andrew Carnegie, who arrived in America as a poor boy and became one of the richest men alive, paid for more than 2,500 public [[@libraries|libraries]] around the world.

{{story:libraries}}

In India, the Tata family set up trusts in the early 1900s that still fund hospitals, science institutes and scholarships. In 2014, India became one of the first countries, and by far the biggest, to make some company giving compulsory. Tiny Mauritius had done it in 2009. Larger companies must now spend 2% of their average profits on causes such as education, health and the environment.

The [[@us-constitution|US Constitution]] came into force in 1789. Two years later, the first ten amendments, known as the Bill of Rights, were added, protecting freedoms like speech and religion. Since then, only 17 more have passed.

Some changed the country deeply. The 13th Amendment ended [[@us-civil-war|slavery]] in 1865. The 19th gave [[@womens-vote|women the vote]] in 1920. The 26th lowered the voting age from 21 to 18 in 1971. Only one, the 18th, which banned alcohol, was later cancelled.

{{story:womens-vote}}

In Mongolia, many herding families live in a ger, a round tent of felt stretched over a wooden frame. A family can take it down, load it onto a few animals or a truck, and put it up again in a few hours. The door traditionally faces south, away from cold winds.

In India, the Raika camel herders of Rajasthan and the Gaddi shepherds of the Himalaya move with their animals and use light shelters on the road. The life of [[@people-nomads-india|India's nomads]] follows the seasons.

{{story:desert-life}}

By the 1840s, surveyors had measured the angles to distant snow peaks in the Himalayas from the plains far below. In 1852, Radhanath Sikdar, a brilliant mathematician who was chief "computer" for the Survey, finished calculations showing that Peak XV was taller than any other mountain known.

It was later named Mount Everest, after the head of the survey, although Everest himself had objected that local people should have their own name used. Today the mountain is also known as Sagarmatha in [[@nepal|Nepal]] and Chomolungma in Tibet.

{{story:mountains-tallest}}

Around 1910, Phalke watched a foreign film called *The Life of Christ* in a Bombay theatre. He was gripped. If Christ's story could be told on a screen, he thought, so could the stories of Krishna and Rama.

He needed money and skills. To persuade backers, he planted peas in a pot and filmed the plant one frame at a time over weeks. Shown at normal speed, the seedling seemed to burst out of the soil. In 1912 he travelled to London, bought a camera and studied how films were made. The pictures that move on a screen are really still frames shown fast, and Phalke now knew [[@movies-move|how movies move]].

{{story:movies-move}}

In 1952 in Helsinki, wrestler Khashaba Dadasaheb Jadhav won bronze, the first individual medal for independent India. He came from a village in Maharashtra and struggled to raise money for the trip.

India then waited 44 years for another individual medal. Leander Paes won bronze in [[@tennis|tennis]] at Atlanta in 1996. In 2000 in Sydney, Karnam Malleswari became the first Indian woman to win an Olympic medal, a bronze in weightlifting. In Athens in 2004, shooter Rajyavardhan Singh Rathore won silver in double trap, independent India's first individual silver.

{{story:speed-distance}}

In *Awaara*, Raj Kapoor plays a boy pushed into a life of crime by poverty. The film asks whether people are born good or bad, or made so by their surroundings. Its title song, "Awaara Hoon" ("I am a vagabond"), became a hit far beyond India. The film was shown at the Cannes Film Festival in 1953.

In *Shree 420*, a poor young man arrives in Bombay and is tempted by easy money. The song "Mera Joota Hai Japani" has him singing that his shoes are Japanese, his trousers English and his hat Russian, but his heart is Indian. Newly independent India loved it.

{{story:film-songs}}

Front crawl, often called freestyle, is the fastest stroke. The arms pull in turn, so there is almost always one arm driving the swimmer forward, while the legs flutter kick.

Butterfly is the second fastest. Both arms swing forward together while the legs make a dolphin-like kick. Backstroke is like front crawl on the back. Breaststroke is the slowest, because the arms and legs recover forward under the water, creating extra drag. Its frog-like kick is still the best stroke for staying afloat and looking around.

{{story:floating-ships}}

In [[@ancient-greece|ancient Greece]], Hippocrates and his followers taught that illness has natural causes, not punishments from gods. Doctors still take an oath named after him. Later, a Greek doctor in Rome named Galen wrote hundreds of works. Human dissection wasn't allowed, so he cut open monkeys and pigs. Some of what he described was right for animals but wrong for humans.

His books were trusted for over 1,300 years. In the 11th century, the Persian scholar Ibn Sina wrote the Canon of Medicine, a giant textbook used in Europe and Asia for centuries.

{{story:ancient-egypt}}

Most organ donation after death happens in hospital intensive care units. Sometimes a person's brain is so badly injured that it has completely and permanently stopped working, even though machines keep their heart beating and lungs breathing. Doctors call this brain death, and they test for it very carefully, following strict laws.

At that point, a counsellor may ask the family whether their loved one would have wanted to donate. Many families say that knowing their loved one helped others gave them some comfort. The organs are removed with care and respect, and rushed to patients chosen by medical need and how well they match. Speed matters. A heart needs to be transplanted within about four to six hours, so cities sometimes clear roads to let an ambulance race through.

{{story:immune-system}}

Pain isn't a simple measure of damage. A football player may not notice a cut until the match ends. A small worry can make a sore throat feel worse. The brain turns the pain signal up or down depending on what else is happening, what you expect and how you feel.

The [[@brain-works|brain]] itself has no pain sensors. That's why surgeons can sometimes operate on the brain while a patient is awake, which helps them avoid harming areas that control speech or movement. Some people who have lost an arm or leg still feel pain where it used to be. This phantom pain shows how much of pain is built by the brain.

{{story:spicy-hot}}

The Arthashastra is long, with 15 books inside it. It explains how to collect [[@taxes|taxes]], run mines and markets, keep weights and measures honest, store grain against famine and protect forests and elephants.

It also has a hard side. It describes networks of spies in disguise, as monks, traders or students, who report on officials and enemies. It discusses secret messages, poisons and how to win wars by tricks as well as battles. Its main aim is a strong, wealthy and safe kingdom, and it is frank about the methods.

{{story:taxes}}

In a park in the middle of LA, sticky tar seeps up from underground at the [[@la-brea|La Brea Tar Pits]]. For tens of thousands of years, animals wandered in and got stuck. Their bones were preserved in the tar.

{{story:la-brea}}

Scientists have found more than a million bones, including mammoths, dire wolves, giant ground sloths and over 2,000 [[@sabre-tooth|sabre-toothed cats]]. Visitors can watch scientists cleaning fossils in a glass-walled lab at the museum.

Many Hindus believe that a dip in the Narmada washes away wrongs, and temples line its banks. The island town of Omkareshwar holds one of the twelve jyotirlingas, the most sacred shrines of Shiva. Smooth, egg-shaped stones from the riverbed are kept in homes and temples as Shiva lingas.

Some devotees walk the Narmada parikrama. They set off from the source, walk down one bank to the sea, cross over and walk all the way back up the other side. The full walk covers thousands of kilometres and can take months. Pilgrims sleep in ashrams and villages, and are fed by strangers along the way.

{{story:ice-age-animals}}

Rockets for those missions were stacked upright inside the Vehicle Assembly Building, about 160 m tall. It is so big inside that, on humid days, wisps of cloud can form near the ceiling. Its doors are among the tallest in the world.

A huge American flag is painted on its side. Each star is about 1.8 m across, and each stripe is nearly as wide as a road lane. Rockets were carried out to the pad on giant tracked crawlers moving at about walking pace, or slower.

{{story:reusable-rockets}}

Holmes was written at a time when science was changing police work. He carries a magnifying glass, studies soil and tobacco ash, and runs chemistry experiments in his sitting room. He even talks about how footprints and handwriting can betray a criminal.

Real detectives at the time were only beginning to use such methods. A few years after the early stories, police in Bengal and then Britain began using [[@fingerprints|fingerprints]] to identify people.

{{story:logic}}

In 1934, Webster's New International Dictionary printed "dord", meaning density. Nobody had ever used it. A note saying "D or d", meaning the letter D in capital or small form could stand for density, got misread as a single word.

"Dord" sat in the dictionary for years until an editor noticed in 1939 that it had no history at all. It was finally removed in 1947. Lexicographers call this a ghost word. Some reference books now plant fake entries on purpose, nicknamed mountweazels, to catch copycats who steal their pages.

{{story:aadhaar}}

[[@sweets-india|Indian sweets]] often start with milk, boiled down for hours into a thick paste called khoa. That becomes barfi, peda and the dough for gulab jamun, which are fried and soaked in syrup. Bengal is famous for spongy rasgullas made from chhena, a fresh cheese.

Both West Bengal and Odisha say the rasgulla was born on their land. In 2017 West Bengal received a GI tag, an official label linking a product to a place, for its "Banglar Rasogolla". In 2019 Odisha got one for "Odisha Rasagola". Both sides are proud, and both sweets are delicious.

{{story:japan-culture}}
